============================================================================================
PART 1 — billing models as implemented in the vendor SDKs
============================================================================================
  braket tracker unavailable: No module named 'braket'

  IonQ (qbraid.runtime.ionq.job) — cost fields exposed on the job
     class CostValue(TypedDict):
     """Type for cost/estimated_cost values."""
     class IonQJobCost(TypedDict):
     """Type for IonQ job cost response."""
     estimated_cost: CostValue
     cost: Optional[CostValue]
     def cost(self) -> IonQJobCost:
     """Return the cost information for the IonQ job."""
     return self.session.get(f"/jobs/{self.id}/cost").json()

  IBM (qiskit-ibm-runtime): billed in QPU SECONDS. rep_delay is a backend
    configuration field; the relevant attributes are
    backend.configuration().rep_delay_range / default_rep_delay,
    and dt (gate time granularity). Requires credentials to query, so the
    numbers below are the published defaults rather than a live read.

============================================================================================
PART 2 — one Heisenberg N=6 gradient priced under each model
============================================================================================

  Heisenberg N=6: M=24, G=3, base 4096 shots/circuit
  IBM documented formula: overhead + (rep_delay + circuit_length) * circuits * shots
    overhead 2.0s, rep_delay 250us, $1.60/s
  Braket: $0.3/task + per-shot, NO gate or depth term

  method               circuits     shots  circ len    QPU s    IBM $     Rigetti $         IQM $        IonQ $
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  parameter-shift           144    589824      5.0us    152.4   243.83        574.04        898.44      17737.92
  QLTO V3 (QPE)               9     36864    142.3us     16.5    26.34         35.88         56.15       1108.62
  QLTO V4 (direct)           17     69632      5.5us     19.8    31.66         67.77        106.07       2094.06

  QLTO V3 (QPE)        vs p-shift:  IBM   9.3x   Rigetti 16.0x  IQM 16.0x  IonQ 16.0x
  QLTO V4 (direct)     vs p-shift:  IBM   7.7x   Rigetti 8.5x  IQM 8.5x  IonQ 8.5x
  V3 vs V4                          IBM   1.2x   Rigetti 1.9x  IQM 1.9x  IonQ 1.9x      (>1 = V3 cheaper)

============================================================================================
PART 3 — where the crossover sits, as a function of rep_delay
============================================================================================
  V3 wins over V4 when its G-fold shot saving beats its extra circuit time:
      G  >  (depth_V3*t_gate + t_read + rep) / (depth_V4*t_gate + t_read + rep)

     rep_delay   V3 per-shot   V4 per-shot    ratio  G needed   G=3 verdict
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
           0us        142.3us          5.5us     26.0      26.0       V4 wins
           1us        143.3us          6.5us     22.2      22.2       V4 wins
          10us        152.3us         15.5us      9.8       9.8       V4 wins
          50us        192.3us         55.5us      3.5       3.5       V4 wins
         250us        392.3us        255.5us      1.5       1.5       V3 wins
         500us        642.3us        505.5us      1.3       1.3       V3 wins

  The whole verdict turns on rep_delay. At rep=0 the deep circuit pays its
  full 26x and V4 wins; at IBM's 250us default the delay dominates both, the
  ratio collapses toward 1, and V3's G-fold circuit saving carries it.

============================================================================================
PART 4 — projection to sizes no simulator reaches
============================================================================================
  Everything above is measured at N<=8, which is not where the question
  lives. Past ~30 qubits a highly entangled circuit cannot be simulated at
  all, so the ONLY way to compare is the analytic cost model - which needs
  no simulator, because circuit count and depth are counting arguments.

  Fitted from v19 (Heisenberg, efficient_su2 reps=1, kappa FIXED at 4):
     M = 4N     G = 3     T = 3(N-1)
     depth  p-shift ~ N+8     V4 ~ N+15     V3 ~ 408N-472
     circuits p-shift = 2MG = 24N    V3 = 2B = 8    V4 = B(G+1) = 16

      N     M  p-shift circ  V3 depth    V3 dur  IBM: p-shift        V3        V4   V3/V4
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      8    32           192      2792      195us           324      29.7      31.7    1.07
     10    40           240      3608      253us           405      33.1      31.7    0.96
     20    80           480      7688      538us           808      49.9      31.8    0.64
     30   120           720     11768      824us          1214      66.8      31.8    0.48
     50   200          1200     19928     1395us          2033     100.5      32.0    0.32
    100   400          2400     40328     2823us          4117     184.7      32.4    0.18

  CORRECTION: the table above holds SHOTS FIXED at 4096 for every N, which
  is wrong and would make V4's cost constant in N - an exponential speedup,
  which is the tell. T4 gives Var(g_i) = (1/S)[a + b(n-1)] with b > 0 for any
  readout that returns an ENERGY (both V3 and V4; only the bounded +-1
  Hadamard bit has b = 0). And Var(H) is EXTENSIVE - the benchmark audit
  measured 8.315 / 14.110 / 21.241 at N = 4/6/8, i.e. Theta(N). Holding
  precision fixed therefore needs S ~ Theta(N).

  Per FULL RUN (20 epochs), shots scaled as S(N) = 4096 * Var(N)/Var(6):
      N  Var(H)    shots p-shift circ  V4 circ   p-shift $      V3 $      V4 $  V4 saving
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      8    21.2     5886         3840      340        9230       765       822        11x
     10    27.7     7676         4800      340       15052      1123      1071        14x
     20    60.0    16627         9600      340       65376      3797      2323        28x
     30    92.3    25579        14400      340      151265      7943      3581        42x
     50   156.9    43481        24000      340      430887     20653      6119        70x
    100   318.4    88238        48000      340     1772523     78197     12582       141x

============================================================================================
PART 5 — the cost I never charged: FIDELITY
============================================================================================
  Every table above prices TIME and treats depth as nearly free because
  rep_delay dominates it. That is true of the BILL and false of the PHYSICS.
  Depth is what fidelity is denominated in, and a circuit that returns noise
  is not cheap at any price.

  Per-circuit two-qubit counts scale as (v16, confirmed at N=6):
      parameter-shift  C_a           ~ N        (5 at N=6)
      QLTO V4          C_a + 2n      ~ 3N       (17 at N=6)  <- the W gate
      QLTO V3          + 2^k*r*T     ~ 158N     (949 at N=6)

  The W gate is the thing V4 pays for its shallowness: one CONTROLLED
  rotation per active parameter, so 2n extra two-qubit gates that
  parameter-shift never pays. Unmitigated survival is (1-p)^cx; zero-noise
  extrapolation costs a shot overhead ~ exp(2*p*cx).

  two-qubit error p = 0.005
      N  p-shift cx   V4 cx    V3 cx  p-shift surv   V4 surv   V3 surv  ZNE p-shift   ZNE V4
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      6           6      18      948         0.970     0.914  8.64e-03          1.1      1.2
     20          20      60     3160         0.905     0.740  1.32e-07          1.2      1.8
     50          50     150     7900         0.778     0.471  6.34e-18          1.6      4.5
    100         100     300    15800         0.606     0.222  4.02e-35          2.7     20.1
    300         300     900    47400         0.222     0.011 6.52e-104         20.1   8103.1

  V3 IS ALREADY DEAD AT N=6 UNMITIGATED - survival 8.6e-03, i.e. the circuit
  returns noise. That was the coherence objection I raised and then walked
  back on billing grounds; the billing argument was right about the INVOICE
  and wrong about whether the answer is usable.

  AND V4'S ADVANTAGE HAS A CEILING. Its circuit-count edge over
  parameter-shift grows ~1.4N, but its mitigation overhead grows as
  exp(2p*3N)/exp(2p*N) = exp(4pN) - EXPONENTIALLY faster. Break-even:
      N= 100   circuit edge     140x   fidelity penalty      7.4x   net    18.9x
      N= 200   circuit edge     280x   fidelity penalty     54.6x   net     5.1x
      N= 300   circuit edge     420x   fidelity penalty    403.4x   net     1.0x
      N= 400   circuit edge     560x   fidelity penalty   2981.0x   net     0.2x
      N= 500   circuit edge     700x   fidelity penalty  22026.5x   net     0.0x

  So the Theta(N) advantage is real only while error is CORRECTED rather
  than mitigated. Unmitigated it peaks near N~300 and inverts after. Under
  fault tolerance the currency changes again to T-count / spacetime volume,
  where the W gate's 2n rotations are the term that matters - UNCOSTED HERE.

  SO NOTHING HERE IS CONSTANT, AND NOTHING IS EXPONENTIAL. Counting orders:
      parameter-shift  Theta(N) circuits x Theta(N) shots x O(1) dur = Theta(N^2)
      QLTO V3          O(1) circuits x Theta(N) shots x Theta(N) dur = Theta(N^2)
      QLTO V4          O(1) circuits x Theta(N) shots x O(1) dur     = Theta(N)
  V4's advantage over parameter-shift is a factor of N - GROWING, POLYNOMIAL,
  and it comes entirely from circuits being O(1) while depth stays under
  rep_delay. V3 loses its edge because its duration grows with N as well.

  V3/V4 > 1 means V3 is cheaper. It falls through 1 where V3's circuit
  DURATION overtakes rep_delay - depth*70ns > 250us, i.e. depth > 3571,
  i.e. N ~ 10 on this fit. Past that V3 pays its depth in full and the
  G-fold circuit saving no longer covers it.

  CAVEAT, and it cuts against V3 harder than the table shows: this holds
  kappa FIXED at 4. QPE resolution is 2*margin*||H||/2^kappa and ||H|| is
  extensive, so holding kappa constant means the gradient degrades as N
  grows. Scaling kappa honestly makes V3 depth ~N*T = O(N^2) and brings the
  crossover in sooner still. The V3 column is therefore optimistic.

  Note also NEITHER arm is tuned or optimised here - no transpiler
  optimisation_level 3, no pulse-level compression, no rep_delay tuning,
  no block-width optimum (T10 says n* ~ 0.65M, i.e. B ~ 1.5 against the 4
  shipped, which is ~2.7x of circuits left on the table for BOTH V3 and V4).
